# MKFS(1M)

## NAME

mkfs - construct a file system

## SYNOPSIS

| **/etc/mkfs** special nblocks [ ninodes ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Mkfs* constructs a file system on the special file *special* by writing a superblock, an empty
i-list and a free list onto it, and leaves it holding one empty directory: its root. *Nblocks*
is the size of the file system to make.

*Ninodes* is the number of i-nodes to leave room for. It is rounded up to fill the last block
of the i-list, and defaults to one i-node per two blocks - 1024 of them on a whole drive, in 32
blocks, or 1.6% of the volume. v7's ratio was one per four blocks and 4.xBSD's one per sixteen;
neither transfers. A block here is 3072 bytes, six times a PDP-11's, so a file that cost six
blocks there costs one here while still costing exactly one i-node, and the natural ratio is
nearer 1:1 than 1:4.

**Note: the prototype-file form is not ported.** v7's *mkfs* took either a proto file - a
bootstrap program, a size, an i-node count, and a recursive listing of the files to put on the
new volume, terminated with **$** - or, if that argument was a string of digits, a bare size.
Only the second is here, and this is the one deliberate divergence in the program. Four
reasons:

- the bootstrap token is meaningless on this machine. The simulator loads the kernel image
  directly and nothing reads block 0; there is no bproc(8) here and none is planned.

- *cfile*(), the recursive half, has a 3072-byte block buffer and a 512-entry indirect block as
  automatics - about 1024 words of frame per directory level, against a stack of 4096 words that
  nothing checks.

- those two buffers are exactly the ones a raw transfer cannot use. See **THE RAW DEVICE** below:
  a buffer handed to the disk must be a 512-word-aligned static, which a recursive function
  cannot have.

- *b6fsutil* on the build host does the same job better, from a manifest, and is how every image
  in this system is in fact populated.

## THE LAYOUT

```
block 0             the bootstrap.  Written by nothing here, and read by nothing.
block 1             the superblock.
blocks 2..isize-1   the i-list.
blocks isize..      data.
```

The first data block is reported when *mkfs* finishes, and is the superblock's *s_isize* -
which is that block number and not a count of i-list blocks.

**Note:** i-node 1 is left unallocated and there is **no bad-blocks i-node.** v7 wrote a dummy
regular file there holding the blocks its *badblk*() had rejected; that routine returned 0
unconditionally even on the PDP-11, the kernel's *ialloc*() will not hand out any i-node below
the root's in any case, and the dummy serves only to give *fsck*(1M) something to be puzzled
by.

**Note:** the free list is built plainly, from the end of the volume down, so that the kernel
allocates upwards. v7's *[ m n ]* arguments and the rotational interleave they described are
gone with the *s_m* and *s_n* fields, which this port's superblock does not have: the pattern
does not survive the first churn of the free list, and this is not a drive whose latency it
would hide.

## BLOCKS HERE ARE 3072 BYTES

**Note:** *nblocks* is a **filesystem** block - 3072 bytes, 512 words of 48 bits - and so is
every number *mkfs* prints. One EC-5052 drive is **2000** of them.

This is deliberately not the 1024-byte block that *df*(1M), du(1), *quot*(1M) and ls(1) report
in. Those programs *measure* a filesystem and a reader of theirs should not have to know its
block size; *mkfs writes* one, and its argument is the superblock's *s_fsize* verbatim. Expect
*df*(1M) to report **three times** the number *mkfs* was given.

## THE RAW DEVICE

**Note:** *mkfs* is the first program on this system that **writes** a raw device, and a raw
transfer here obeys four conditions that v7 knew nothing of: the buffer must begin at byte 0 of
a word, the count must be a whole number of filesystem blocks, the buffer's word address must
be a multiple of 512, and the seek must be block-aligned. The first three fail with **EFAULT**
or **EIO**; the fourth is silent and reads or writes the wrong block. *Mkfs* therefore has one
buffer, aligned at startup, and every transfer names it. See *cmd/mkfs/README.md* in the source
tree, and *cmd/df/README.md* for the conditions themselves.

## SUPER-USER ONLY

**Note:** *mkfs* writes the raw disk, and */dev/rmd0* is mode 0600 because that one node is the
contents of every file on the volume. So this program works for the super-user and for nobody
else. It is deliberately **not** set-user-id, and the reason is sharper than *df*(1M)'s: a
set-uid *mkfs* would let anybody destroy every file on a volume with one command.

## WARNING

*Mkfs* does not know which volume the system is running from, and nothing stops you naming it.
*mkfs /dev/rmd0* destroys the root filesystem while it is mounted. There is no check to be
made: the raw major and the block major are different numbers and nothing on this machine maps
one to the other. v7 had no guard either.

## FILES

*/dev/rmd1* - the second drive, and the only volume on this system it is safe to name. There is
one drive per *minor* number, bit 5 the controller, bits 4-3 the group and bits 2-0 the drive;
there are no partitions.

## DIAGNOSTICS

**a filesystem needs at least 8 blocks** and **the i-list does not leave room for any data
blocks** are the two size checks, and both happen before anything is written.

**cannot read block** *n* where *n* is one less than *nblocks*: *mkfs* reads the last block
before writing the first, which is how it finds out whether the drive is that big without
knowing the drive's size. An *errno* of 5 there means the device refused the block number - the
volume is smaller than *nblocks* - and a count of 0 means the special is an ordinary file that
is too short.

**cannot write block** *n* with an *errno* of 14 means one of the four conditions in *THE RAW
DEVICE* is broken; 5 means the driver refused the transfer.

**out of free space** cannot happen and means the free list this program has just built is
inconsistent.

The superblock is written **last**, which is the commit point: a *mkfs* that fails partway
leaves a volume with no magic number, which nothing will mount, rather than one that looks
plausible.

## BUGS

The data area is not zeroed, so a volume made over an old file system still holds its blocks
until they are allocated. Nothing leaks through the file system - the kernel's *alloc*() clears
a block on the way out - but the raw device still has them.

**Note:** v7's entry here was that there should be some way to specify links. It went with the
prototype language.

## SEE ALSO

filsys(5), dir(5), df(1M), quot(1M)

**Note:** v7's SEE ALSO named bproc(8), which does not exist here - see the bootstrap block
above - and *fsck*(1M), which is not ported yet.
